      "document_hash": "828eb19c3516b2fa43b6e5ae6de9a3c7103437a21a83b2093eae7b5f527e7387",
      "evidence_id": "ev-828eb19c3516-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:04 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "VANESSA F. MALONE, ESQ., on behalf of the Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2018-17-8995",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "828eb19c3516b2fa43b6e5ae6de9a3c7103437a21a83b2093eae7b5f527e7387",
      "evidence_id": "ev-828eb19c3516-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. MALONE: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: When Congress uses plain, ordinary words in a statute, the words should be read accordingly. The language of Section 3624(e)'s tolling provision is both plain and unambiguous."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2018-17-8995",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "828eb19c3516b2fa43b6e5ae6de9a3c7103437a21a83b2093eae7b5f527e7387",
      "evidence_id": "ev-828eb19c3516-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: It was treated as if it were part of the sentence?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "VANESSA F. MALONE, ESQ., on behalf of the Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2018-17-8995",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "828eb19c3516b2fa43b6e5ae6de9a3c7103437a21a83b2093eae7b5f527e7387",
      "evidence_id": "ev-828eb19c3516-0000-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In Ohio, what happens when a defendant is sentenced is that the state judge is required to apportion pretrial or official detention towards the credit of the sentence. It is not imprisonment. It is a credit that goes towards the days that a person has to spend in imprisonment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Jenny Ellickson (Respondent)",
      "case_id": "2018-17-8995",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "828eb19c3516b2fa43b6e5ae6de9a3c7103437a21a83b2093eae7b5f527e7387",
      "evidence_id": "ev-828eb19c3516-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Petitioner was not serving his federal term of supervised release during the 10 months when he was sitting in state jail between his arrest and sentencing for new crimes. That conclusion follows from the plain text of Section 3624(e), which broadly tolls supervised release during periods of imprisonment in connection with a conviction."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2018-17-8995",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "828eb19c3516b2fa43b6e5ae6de9a3c7103437a21a83b2093eae7b5f527e7387",
      "evidence_id": "ev-828eb19c3516-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Thank you, counsel."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2018-17-8995",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "828eb19c3516b2fa43b6e5ae6de9a3c7103437a21a83b2093eae7b5f527e7387",
      "evidence_id": "ev-828eb19c3516-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Justice KAGAN: I mean, \"in connection with,\" as I say, I totally accept your point that it's very broad, but the question is whether it can effectively change the tense of the statute just because it's so broad."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Jenny Ellickson (Respondent)",
      "case_id": "2018-17-8995",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "828eb19c3516b2fa43b6e5ae6de9a3c7103437a21a83b2093eae7b5f527e7387",
      "evidence_id": "ev-828eb19c3516-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. ELLICKSON: The question is just what the default should be. And Congress determined that for defendants who are convicted, the default should be that they have their supervised release terms tolled while they're in jail. For defendants who are not convicted, Congress set a different default. The default is no -- is no tolling, but, because of the supervised release scheme, the federal judge has additional tools that he or she can deploy to potentially add on additional supervised release if necessary."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2018-17-8995",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "828eb19c3516b2fa43b6e5ae6de9a3c7103437a21a83b2093eae7b5f527e7387",
      "evidence_id": "ev-828eb19c3516-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: I'm sorry. Just -- so, in this case, the way you interpret what happened, if Mr. Mont had done that during the term of pretrial detention, that would not be a ground for revocation of -- of supervised --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2018-17-8995",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "828eb19c3516b2fa43b6e5ae6de9a3c7103437a21a83b2093eae7b5f527e7387",
      "evidence_id": "ev-828eb19c3516-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: So let's assume the following hypothetical: Defendant is arrested for drug charges. He's later acquitted, but while in jail, he now commits a drug offense."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
